{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact S1 head 0abdac41978a7fb70f22bdcc8901fdd04b5fd676 on builder/state-s1-transport-20261107, tree 92aad036, all eleven paths, for request e39e7cef under promise 54aa90b3 and review request f6a84d70. That includes the three source seals in scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs. Each hosted MCP transport generation now has one owner, and every authority fence that main had still runs, in main's order. I found one row outcome that differs from main. No hosted path reaches it today, so it does not block this head. A later head must fix it (finding F1 below).\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main 7244d884 is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the eleven artifact paths: hosted.ts, http.ts and oauth-http.ts in packages/mcp/src, two scope tests, the seal test, the note and four manifests. The commit-message check passes: 12 checked, 0 flagged. No dependency, lockfile, gate script, grant, wire type or Wrangler file changes.\n- The seal change. Commit 42fabdd3 changes only the three literals for http.ts, oauth-http.ts and hosted.ts, and keeps every route and gate assertion. The patch file hashes to 78793434…, the value in planner act 3655896d. I recomputed each seal outside the builder's worktree with the test's own method, a SHA-256 of the cleaned rolldown syntax tree. Main's three sources give exactly the three old literals, and the head's sources give exactly the three new ones. The evidence records Hugh's direct approval of that exact patch, and 3655896d assigns the seal review to me.\n- The gate. I read gate.exit at the gated source 446e50d4, tree c179e22f: \"exit 0\". result.json records Node 135, Counting 56, Plain 4,016 with one skip and Native 1,113 with two skips, with no report error and no unhandled error. before.json and after.json are byte-equal: clean status, 4,201 tracked inputs, lockfile 5a717d43 and gate.sh 001c4b10, which equal the files at 446e50d4. receipt.json records unchanged source and no owned process left. Between 446e50d4 and this head, only the note, the gate manifest and the merged sprint report change, so docs/testing.md needs no new gate.\n- The evidence. The four manifests hash to the SHA-256 values that the note cites. Their 149 files match their sizes and digests and are read-only, and the credential scan finds no match. The characterization case fails by assertion on baseline 80a91d4e: the restored TEST-late-abrupt row is absent. Both controls fail by assertion. Without the authority fence, the replacement bearer's row disappears. With abrupt changed to graceful, the retained row is absent. The held-loader case passes on main, as the note says, so it guards against a regression rather than showing a difference.\n- The tests. No existing case, assertion or deadline is removed. The OAuth transport case now passes the layer's generation and builds its late layer inside the factory; its order and null assertions do not change. http.test.ts and http-idle.test.ts do not change.\n- Semantics. An independent read of the three sources against main, with a probe that runs the real http.ts and oauth-http.ts of both revisions on stubbed ports, found these preserved:\n  - authority: openHttp still builds the layer, checks the epoch and the current candidate (now also `generation.closed`), and only then restores rows and installs the service. Dispatch re-runs `#authorized` and the row comparison. Every epoch increment still clears the transport. A held loader under an old bearer cannot restore rows, install its service or replace the new transport, and its request gets 401;\n  - retirement: a late result or late attach on a closed generation does nothing, `#persist` and `acceptIssue` check `closed`, and a late rejection keeps the original disposition;\n  - row outcomes for DELETE, idle expiry, hard expiry, a refused initialize, bearer rotation, grant end, retire, eviction and refresh, except F1;\n  - timers: old timers act only on their own generation, and a held standing read cannot extend its previous deadline. Graceful close now also cancels the standing timer;\n  - limits: STREAMS_MOST, the per-session pending count, SESSIONS_MOST, OAUTH_GRANTS_MOST and the write tails;\n  - every client-visible status code, OAuth challenge, final SSE event and listen result.\n\nThe intended change holds. On main, a late abrupt opening deletes its restored row. On the head, the row stays and `open()` returns null. In HostedAgent the check at hosted.ts line 746 runs before restoration, so main's deletion was reachable only from a test factory that restores before it is held.\n\nF1 (does not block; a later head must fix it). A layer that a refresh detached can still be closed gracefully, and the graceful close deletes the rows that the detach kept. On main, `#close` returned the first close (`if (layer.closing) return layer.closing`), so a second close returned the detach result. On the head, `#close` (oauth-http.ts line 87) calls `owner.graceful()` by default. `graceful()` (http.ts line 375) still runs after a detach and removes every session row. The map entry goes only in a later `.finally`. Two callers can reach the detached layer: the sweep of non-current layers in `open()` (oauth-http.ts line 28) and `closeAll()`. The probe shows three cases, each keeping the row on main and deleting it on the head:\n- a detach, then `closeAll()` in the same tick;\n- a detach, then another grant's `open()` sweep in the same tick;\n- a detach while another sweep holds an older copy of the layer list.\n\nF1 does not block for three reasons:\n- In HostedAgent, the `rotated` hook calls `closeGrant(id, true)` inside the token request. A sweep runs inside a different request.\n- Row removal is synchronous SQL, so a sweep finishes without I/O, and no other request can run inside it.\n- `closeAll()` runs only on retire, which deletes every oauth_session row in SQL first.\n\nThe effect, if reached, is a lost legacy session: the client gets 404 and initializes again. It grants no access. Fix it in the next head that changes oauth-http.ts. In `#close`, return `owner.disposed()` when `owner.closed` is already true, and add a witness that detaches a layer and then closes it, with the row still present. That fix also removes the need for the \"existing\" special case at line 22. It changes oauth-http.ts, so its seal changes again.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One generation now owns the opening, the HTTP resource, sessions, streams, rows, write tails and both timers. The three transitions are named, so a caller states which outcome it wants. Authority stays with HostedAgent's two epochs, and a generation cannot override them. F1 comes from `#close` treating every close as a new decision instead of keeping the first one.\n\nSecurity: no finding. Every authority fence runs as on main, and no request under a retired or rotated bearer gains access or keeps a session that main would end. The seal values are the parser's real output for the reviewed sources. F1 can delete a session row early; it cannot keep or grant one.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Four notes:\n\n1. The closed branch of `HttpGeneration.restore` (http.ts lines 268 to 277) cannot be reached from HostedAgent, because hosted.ts line 746 checks `closed` first. A throw there would make the state machine smaller.\n2. Only the test accessor `inside()` reads `phase` as opening or open.\n3. The `OAuthHttpLayer.deadline` getter duplicates `generation.deadline`.\n4. `scheduleStanding` takes the scheduler on every call. A constructor port would remove that argument.\n\nThis approval authorizes no deployment and closes no live acceptance.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cd02c0954e2685a7820bc0e3fe4cbc3fff150829","binding":"assigned","head":"0abdac41978a7fb70f22bdcc8901fdd04b5fd676","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d819ba4ed693dba3315a5cafc187ad1b7448b271\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:54aa90b3949bebb3ced81c8a4eb0bc65b27a0c33\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f99a8ec2e95640f5e1e4de1d9049073385f5d6a7","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}